WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.175
Most of this module is about hazards in mining

00:07.175 --> 00:09.963
in general. In this video we look at what has actually

00:09.963 --> 00:12.888
hurt people at our own operations. All of it comes

00:12.888 --> 00:16.275
from MSHA's public records, as of mid-October two

00:16.275 --> 00:17.812
thousand twenty-six.

00:18.924 --> 00:22.099
MSHA's records show twenty-one reportable injuries

00:22.099 --> 00:24.837
at Boonesboro since two thousand sixteen.

00:24.837 --> 00:27.662
At the old Clover Bottom mine, twenty-three since

00:27.662 --> 00:30.099
Allen took it over in two thousand four.

00:30.099 --> 00:32.899
At the newer Clover Bottom mine, none on record

00:32.899 --> 00:35.224
since it opened in two thousand twenty-one.

00:35.224 --> 00:37.824
And at Dix River, two since Allen took over in

00:37.824 --> 00:40.949
two thousand eighteen. Those time periods are different,

00:40.949 --> 00:43.274
so don't use these numbers to compare one site

00:43.274 --> 00:45.962
to another. Use them to see the patterns.

00:47.107 --> 00:49.657
The surprise is that most of these injuries didn't

00:49.657 --> 00:52.569
come from explosions or major failures.

00:52.569 --> 00:55.394
They came from ordinary tasks. Climbing down from

00:55.394 --> 00:58.244
equipment. Handling steel and plates.

00:58.244 --> 01:00.869
Using hand tools. Walking on steps.

01:00.869 --> 01:03.257
That's where careful habits count the most.

01:06.581 --> 01:08.693
Slips and falls of people were at the top of the

01:08.693 --> 01:11.681
list at both Boonesboro and the old Clover Bottom

01:11.681 --> 01:14.968
mine, six at each. Missing a step climbing down

01:14.968 --> 01:18.293
a haul truck or loader. Slipping on loose rock.

01:18.293 --> 01:20.856
Stepping into an opening. At Dix River,

01:20.856 --> 01:23.193
both injuries on record were falls while stepping

01:23.193 --> 01:26.305
down at the end of a shift: one on the office steps,

01:26.305 --> 01:27.868
one getting out of a truck.

01:29.014 --> 01:31.627
The control is simple. Face the machine.

01:31.627 --> 01:34.040
Keep three points of contact all the way to the

01:34.040 --> 01:36.740
ground. And remember that the shift isn't over

01:36.740 --> 01:38.227
until you're in your vehicle.

01:39.352 --> 01:42.390
Handling materials was next, six at Boonesboro

01:42.390 --> 01:44.828
and five at the old Clover Bottom mine.

01:44.828 --> 01:47.940
Plates, drill steel, and crane parts that slipped

01:47.940 --> 01:50.578
or swung. Hand tools were behind another eight

01:50.578 --> 01:53.790
between those two sites: a scaling bar glancing

01:53.790 --> 01:57.190
off rock, a pipe wrench slipping, a razor knife,

01:57.190 --> 02:00.315
a sledgehammer pin that broke. Use the right tool,

02:00.315 --> 02:02.765
inspect it, and keep other people out of the line

02:02.765 --> 02:04.953
a tool or load can travel.

02:06.070 --> 02:08.795
Ground falls are fewer, but they're the most serious

02:08.795 --> 02:11.482
pattern. At the old Clover Bottom mine,

02:11.482 --> 02:14.257
four times rock fell from the face or the back,

02:14.257 --> 02:17.032
each time during scaling, checking a room,

02:17.032 --> 02:19.920
or preparing a shot. Boonesboro has one:

02:19.920 --> 02:22.083
rock fell on a miner who walked toward the face

02:22.083 --> 02:25.107
to pick up a lost tooth. We cover this fully in

02:25.107 --> 02:28.083
Module 7. The short version is that you stay out

02:28.083 --> 02:30.845
from under loose ground, and you scale only if

02:30.845 --> 02:32.558
you're trained and authorized.

02:33.688 --> 02:35.663
Then there are machines and haulage.

02:35.663 --> 02:38.675
At Boonesboro, a worker had a hand on a tail pulley

02:38.675 --> 02:41.500
to reach set screws while someone else turned it.

02:41.500 --> 02:44.000
Another miner was riding on the outside of a loader

02:44.000 --> 02:46.513
with his legs hanging off, and the step caught

02:46.513 --> 02:49.826
his leg. The controls are lockout, tag out,

02:49.826 --> 02:52.400
and block motion before your hands go near anything

02:52.400 --> 02:55.050
that can move. Ride only in a seat built for a

02:55.050 --> 02:58.838
passenger. And never remove, open, or reach around

02:58.838 --> 02:59.650
a guard.

03:02.990 --> 03:05.815
Since two thousand twenty-one, MSHA inspectors

03:05.815 --> 03:08.765
have issued forty-four citations at Boonesboro,

03:08.765 --> 03:11.428
thirty-nine at the old Clover Bottom mine,

03:11.428 --> 03:14.728
eleven at the new one, and twenty-four at Dix River.

03:14.728 --> 03:18.278
At Boonesboro, Clover Bottom, and Dix River alike,

03:18.278 --> 03:20.753
one standard is near the top of the list:

03:20.753 --> 03:23.978
equipment defects that affect safety must be corrected

03:23.978 --> 03:26.815
in a timely manner, and unsafe equipment must be

03:26.815 --> 03:28.365
taken out of service.

03:29.505 --> 03:31.654
That tells us about your pre-shift check.

03:31.654 --> 03:33.654
If you see something wrong with a machine,

03:33.654 --> 03:36.329
report it, and don't run it until it's fixed.

03:37.454 --> 03:40.204
Other frequent citations are just as practical.

03:40.204 --> 03:42.504
Moving parts like pulleys and couplings must be

03:42.504 --> 03:45.816
guarded, so report a missing or damaged guard.

03:45.816 --> 03:48.716
Electrical equipment needs to be in safe condition,

03:48.716 --> 03:52.266
so report damaged cords, cables, and boxes,

03:52.266 --> 03:55.104
and leave electrical repairs to qualified people.

03:55.104 --> 03:57.866
And underground, every person has to be accounted

03:57.866 --> 04:00.266
for with a check-in and check-out record.

04:00.266 --> 04:03.291
Tag in and out of the right mine, every time,

04:03.291 --> 04:05.279
and never for anyone else.

04:06.392 --> 04:08.605
What does a good pre-shift check look like?

04:08.605 --> 04:10.780
Walk around the machine before you get in.

04:10.780 --> 04:13.442
Look at the tires, the steps and handrails,

04:13.442 --> 04:16.542
the guards, and the ground underneath for leaks.

04:16.542 --> 04:19.167
Then, from the seat, test the brakes,

04:19.167 --> 04:22.105
the horn, and the backup alarm. Brakes,

04:22.105 --> 04:25.192
horns, and backup alarms show up in the citations

04:25.192 --> 04:28.192
at Clover Bottom and Dix River. If anything isn't

04:28.192 --> 04:30.880
right, report it, and don't operate the machine

04:30.880 --> 04:32.154
until it's fixed.

04:33.279 --> 04:36.154
One more pattern is in the Boonesboro and Dix River

04:36.154 --> 04:39.279
records: electrical condition. Insulating mats

04:39.279 --> 04:41.504
at switchboards need to stay in place,

04:41.504 --> 04:44.179
grounds need to be tested, and damaged cords and

04:44.179 --> 04:47.316
boxes need to be reported. Only qualified people

04:47.316 --> 04:49.741
work on electrical equipment. If you're not one

04:49.741 --> 04:52.841
of them, you stay out, and you tell your supervisor.

04:56.172 --> 04:58.660
The rest of this module teaches the controls that

04:58.660 --> 05:02.085
fit these patterns. Take hazards away if you can.

05:02.085 --> 05:04.285
Guard or lock out what you can't remove.

05:04.285 --> 05:07.397
Follow procedures. And wear your protective equipment,

05:07.397 --> 05:10.010
which is the last layer, not the first.

05:11.167 --> 05:15.405
Parts 56 and 57 require a competent person to examine

05:15.405 --> 05:18.405
each working place before miners begin work there,

05:18.405 --> 05:20.867
so hazards get found and corrected.

05:20.867 --> 05:23.230
Your own look when you arrive backs that up.

05:23.230 --> 05:26.605
A cord left across a walkway, a missing berm,

05:26.605 --> 05:29.305
loose rock, an opening in a catwalk.

05:29.305 --> 05:31.505
Those are exactly the kinds of conditions in the

05:31.505 --> 05:32.980
records you just heard.

05:34.104 --> 05:36.929
When you spot a hazard, you do three things.

05:36.929 --> 05:39.742
Stay clear of it. Keep others away if you can.

05:39.742 --> 05:42.067
And tell your supervisor. You don't have to fix

05:42.067 --> 05:44.117
it yourself, and you shouldn't try unless you're

05:44.117 --> 05:45.892
trained and authorized to.

05:47.010 --> 05:49.598
Why show you all of this? Not to scare you.

05:49.598 --> 05:52.285
Every item in these records is somebody's bad day

05:52.285 --> 05:55.260
at one of our own sites, and each one has a lesson

05:55.260 --> 05:57.673
that's already been paid for. You get to learn

05:57.673 --> 06:00.585
it for free. At the newer Clover Bottom mine,

06:00.585 --> 06:03.135
there are no reportable injuries on record.

06:03.135 --> 06:05.385
That's a record worth keeping, and it's kept by

06:05.385 --> 06:07.160
the same small habits.

06:08.305 --> 06:11.717
To sum up. At our operations, ordinary tasks hurt

06:11.717 --> 06:14.730
people far more often than rare events do.

06:14.730 --> 06:17.555
Stepping down safely, handling steel and tools

06:17.555 --> 06:20.442
carefully, locking out, keeping guards on,

06:20.442 --> 06:23.279
reporting defects, and staying out from under loose

06:23.279 --> 06:26.067
ground prevent most of what's in these records.

06:26.067 --> 06:28.267
They aren't big gestures, but they're the ones

06:28.267 --> 06:29.267
that matter.

06:30.390 --> 06:33.365
Your site's plans always override this video.
